<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  Mono-spinner UAV — Gazebo Harmonic (gz-sim 8) model.

  Parameters from Bernardes, Boyer & Viollet, "Modelling, control and simulation
  of a single rotor UAV with swashplateless torque modulation", Aerospace Science
  and Technology, 2023.  Table 1 unless noted.

  FRAME CONVENTION (matches the paper's Figure 2):
    base_link origin  = F_B, the MAIN BODY centre of mass.  All poses below are
                        expressed relative to it, so every number here can be
                        read straight off the paper.
    battery  at  z = -h_S = -0.145 m   (F_S, below F_B)
    rotor    at  z = +h_P = +0.083 m   (F_P, above F_B)

  The composite centre of mass, which Gazebo computes itself, sits at
  z = -Delta_m/m = -0.06839 m, i.e. 68.4 mm BELOW base_link's origin.
  Nothing in this file needs to know that; the plugin works in base_link frame.

  ONE CORRECTION TO THE PAPER: Table 1 prints Jpz = 0.0001061 kg m^2, which
  violates the inertia triangle inequality (Jpz <= 2*Jpd) by a factor of ~10.
  Corrected to Jpz = 2*Jpd = 1.04e-5 here (equality, Jpd unchanged at 5.2e-6) 
  gz-math's MassMatrix3::ValidMoments() enforces this inequality with
  essentially zero tolerance, so 1.061e-5 (the initial, still-too-high
  correction) was rejected outright.  Do not "fix" this back.
-->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <model name="monospinner">
    <pose>0 0 0.2 0 0 0</pose>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         MAIN BODY  (B_b) — structure, electronics, stator, anti-torque fins
         ================================================================== -->
    <link name="base_link">
      <inertial>
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>0.2100</mass> 
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0010026</ixx>
          <iyy>0.0010026</iyy>
          <izz>0.0001831</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial> <!-- check -->

      <!-- Single collision primitive covering the whole airframe envelope.
           Radius = fin tip radius.  Never use mesh collision on this vehicle. -->
      <collision name="base_collision">
        <pose>0 0 -0.040 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.095</radius><length>0.260</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- Cylindrical fuselage ALL VISUAL -->
      <visual name="fuselage_visual" >
        <pose>0 0 -0.050 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.045</radius><length>0.150</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.15 0.15 0.15 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.20 0.20 0.20 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- Motor mount platform ALL VISUAL -->
      <visual name="platform_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0.045 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.045</radius><length>0.004</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.10 0.10 0.10 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.15 0.15 0.15 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- Brushless motor stator ALL VISUAL -->
      <visual name="motor_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0.065 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.014</radius><length>0.030</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.45 0.45 0.48 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.60 0.60 0.63 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- ================= ANTI-TORQUE FINS ================= ALL VISUAL
           8 fins, 136 mm x 50 mm (paper, Fig. 1 caption).
           Assumed: 136 mm is the vertical height, 50 mm the radial chord.
           Mounted from r = 0.045 to r = 0.095.
           These are VISUAL ONLY.  Their aerodynamic effect enters the plugin
           through K_z, which was measured on the 8-fin prototype.
           ==================================================== -->
      <visual name="fin_0">
        <pose>0.070 0 -0.050 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.050 0.002 0.136</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.85 0.82 0.35 1</ambient><diffuse>0.90 0.87 0.40 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="fin_1">
        <pose>0.0495 0.0495 -0.050 0 0 0.7853982</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.050 0.002 0.136</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.85 0.82 0.35 1</ambient><diffuse>0.90 0.87 0.40 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="fin_2">
        <pose>0 0.070 -0.050 0 0 1.5707963</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.050 0.002 0.136</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.85 0.82 0.35 1</ambient><diffuse>0.90 0.87 0.40 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="fin_3">
        <pose>-0.0495 0.0495 -0.050 0 0 2.3561945</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.050 0.002 0.136</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.85 0.82 0.35 1</ambient><diffuse>0.90 0.87 0.40 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="fin_4">
        <pose>-0.070 0 -0.050 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.050 0.002 0.136</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.85 0.82 0.35 1</ambient><diffuse>0.90 0.87 0.40 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="fin_5">
        <pose>-0.0495 -0.0495 -0.050 0 0 0.7853982</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.050 0.002 0.136</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.85 0.82 0.35 1</ambient><diffuse>0.90 0.87 0.40 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="fin_6">
        <pose>0 -0.070 -0.050 0 0 1.5707963</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.050 0.002 0.136</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.85 0.82 0.35 1</ambient><diffuse>0.90 0.87 0.40 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="fin_7">
        <pose>0.0495 -0.0495 -0.050 0 0 2.3561945</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.050 0.002 0.136</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.85 0.82 0.35 1</ambient><diffuse>0.90 0.87 0.40 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>

      <!-- ================= SENSORS =================
           All four co-located at F_B, per the paper's statement that keeping the
           main CoM close to the electronics and inboard IMUs was a design goal.

           >>> VERIFY THIS BLOCK against
           >>> PX4-Autopilot/Tools/simulation/gz/models/x500/model.sdf
           >>> before first run.  gz_bridge matches on sensor NAME; a mismatch
           >>> gives you a vehicle that spawns and arms but has no estimator.

           NOTE for config/monospinner.params: these sensors sit 68.4 mm ABOVE
           the composite CoM.  In PX4's FRD body frame that is
           EKF2_IMU_POS_Z = -0.0684.  On a body spinning at ~18 rad/s the
           centripetal term is not negligible.
           =========================================== -->
      <!-- Superseded by the ImuPhysical plugin below (reads link kinematics from the ECM
           directly and publishes straight to PX4's hardcoded IMU topic, see CLAUDE.md's
           discussion + src/gz_imu_realism_plugin). Kept here, commented out, as a plain
           (non-nested) sensor block: to actually switch the vehicle onto it, uncomment
           this block AND the ImuPhysical <plugin> below must be commented out AND
           gz-sim-imu-system must be uncommented in worlds/monospinner_default.sdf, since
           this sensor produces no data on its own otherwise (that world-file comment is
           what originally superseded it). Noise values below are also what ImuPhysical's
           <gyro_noise_stddev>/<accel_noise_stddev> were ported from; keep them in sync if
           you change one. Noise modeled after IIM42653, copied from PX4-Autopilot/Tools/
           simulation/gz/models/x500_base/model.sdf (gyro: 0.05 deg/s -> rad/s; accel X/Y:
           0.65 mg-rms -> m/s^2; accel Z: 0.70 mg-rms -> m/s^2). -->
      <!--
      <sensor name="imu_sensor" type="imu">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>250</update_rate>
        <imu>
          <angular_velocity>
            <x><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0.0</mean><stddev>0.0008726646</stddev></noise></x>
            <y><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0.0</mean><stddev>0.0008726646</stddev></noise></y>
            <z><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0.0</mean><stddev>0.0008726646</stddev></noise></z>
          </angular_velocity>
          <linear_acceleration>
            <x><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0.0</mean><stddev>0.00637</stddev></noise></x>
            <y><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0.0</mean><stddev>0.00637</stddev></noise></y>
            <z><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0.0</mean><stddev>0.00686</stddev></noise></z>
          </linear_acceleration>
        </imu>
      </sensor>
      -->

      <sensor name="air_pressure_sensor" type="air_pressure"> <!--air pressure is in the right place-->
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>50</update_rate>
        <air_pressure>
          <pressure>
            <noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.01</stddev></noise>
          </pressure>
        </air_pressure>
      </sensor>

      <!-- Superseded by the MagnetometerPhysical plugin below (src/gz_magnetometer_realism_plugin),
           which computes the field from base_link's true attitude and publishes straight to
           the topic GZBridge::subscribeMag() hardcodes. Kept here, commented out, as a plain
           (non-nested) sensor block: to switch back, uncomment it AND comment out the
           MagnetometerPhysical <plugin> below (two publishers on one topic would interleave
           good and bad samples). gz-sim-magnetometer-system can stay in the world file; with
           no magnetometer sensor in the model it does nothing.

           Why it was replaced (notes from when this sensor was in use):
           DELIBERATE 5.97 deg yaw (0.1042 rad), compensating a sim declination mismatch.
             gz-sim 8's magnetometer system computes the field from <spherical_coordinates>
             (a world <magnetic_field> is ignored tested) and, after GZBridge's axis swap
             (PX4-Autopilot/src/modules/simulation/gz_bridge/GZBridge.cpp:400-404), PX4 sees
             declination -2.56 deg at this world's lat/lon. PX4's own WMM
             (src/lib/world_magnetic_model, used by EKF2 with the default EKF2_DECL_TYPE)
             gives +3.406 deg there. This yaw makes the two agree ONLY WHILE THE VEHICLE IS
             LEVEL: a rotation about the tilted body z is not a rotation about the world
             vertical, so it adds a heading error of about 2 deg at 10 deg of tilt.
             The magnetometer is wrong under tilt anyway, independently of this pose: gz-sim
             8.15.0 hands the sensor NED field components as if they were ENU world axes
             (src/systems/magnetometer/Magnetometer.cc lines 141 and 485 to 506), and
             GZBridge's axis swap only undoes that for level attitudes and yaw. Measured in
             run build/runs/2026-10-02T13-52-43: at 10 deg of tilt the field seen through the
             true attitude is off by 15 to 32 deg in heading, EKF2 then rejects the
             magnetometer and yaw drifts on the gyro alone. Location-dependent: recompute
             if the world's lat/lon changes.
           Noise below: copied from PX4-Autopilot/Tools/simulation/gz/models/x500_base/model.sdf
           (its comment says "3 mgauss RMS, modeled after IIS2MDC"). -->
      <!--
      <sensor name="magnetometer_sensor" type="magnetometer">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0.1042</pose>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>100</update_rate>
        <magnetometer>
          <x><noise type="gaussian"><stddev>0.0001</stddev></noise></x>
          <y><noise type="gaussian"><stddev>0.0001</stddev></noise></y>
          <z><noise type="gaussian"><stddev>0.0001</stddev></noise></z>
        </magnetometer>
      </sensor>
      -->

      <sensor name="navsat_sensor" type="navsat"> <!--gps is in the right place-->
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>30</update_rate>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         POWER SUPPLY  (B_s) — battery, h_S = 0.145 m below F_B
         Kept as its own link so h_S stays an explicit, editable number.
         Note Jsx != Jsy: the battery is NOT axisymmetric.
         ================================================================== -->
    <link name="battery">
      <pose>0 0 -0.145 0 0 0</pose> <!-- This is correct -->
      <inertial>
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>0.2007</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0001794</ixx>
          <iyy>0.0000387</iyy>
          <izz>0.0001928</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="battery_visual">
        <geometry><box><size>0.062 0.040 0.030</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.55 0.40 0.10 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.75 0.55 0.15 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <joint name="battery_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>battery</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         ROTOR  (B_p) — h_P = 0.083 m above F_B

         This link is a MASS CARRIER and a VISUAL ANCHOR ONLY.  It is welded by
         a fixed joint and carries no rotor dynamics: all thrust, reaction
         torque and gyroscopic coupling are applied by the plugin as wrenches on
         base_link.  It exists because m_p and J_p^B are part of the paper's
         composite inertia J (Eq. 6) and dropping them would change the model.

         Its inertia is the azimuth-averaged tensor of Eq. 3-4,
         diag(Jpd, Jpd, Jpz) — which is what makes welding it at alpha = 0
         equivalent to the paper's dJ/dt = 0 assumption.

         DO NOT convert this to a revolute joint.  A dynamically spinning link
         would double-count the gyroscopic term the plugin already applies.
         ================================================================== -->
    <link name="rotor">
      <pose>0 0 0.083 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>0.0067</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0000052</ixx>   <!-- Jpd -->
          <iyy>0.0000052</iyy>   <!-- Jpd -->
          <izz>0.0000104</izz>   <!-- Jpz = 2*Jpd, corrected; see header -->
          <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="hub_visual">
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.008</radius><length>0.012</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.30 0.30 0.32 1</ambient><diffuse>0.45 0.45 0.48 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="blade_visual">
        <geometry><box><size>0.180 0.014 0.002</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.08 0.08 0.08 1</ambient><diffuse>0.12 0.12 0.12 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <joint name="rotor_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>rotor</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         ACTUATOR PLUGIN

         Applies, on base_link:
           thrust      f_p * r          as a force at hub_offset  (Gazebo
                                        derives the h_P e_z x f_p lever torque)
           reaction    k * f_p * r      explicit torque, along the TILTED axis r
           gyroscopic  -gamma_dot * Jpz * (omega x r)   explicit torque
           fin drag    -sign(wz) * Kz * wz^2 * e_z      explicit torque

         where r = [sa*cb, sa*sb, ca] is the thrust direction in base_link frame.
         ================================================================== -->
    <plugin filename="gz_monospinner_plugin" name="monospinner::ThrustVectorActuator">

      <!-- Wrenches are applied to this link; all offsets are in its frame. -->
      <link_name>base_link</link_name>
      <rotor_visual_link>rotor</rotor_visual_link>

      <!-- Geometry (paper Table 1) -->
      <h_P>0.083</h_P>                    <!-- rotor above F_B, m -->
      <h_S>0.145</h_S>                    <!-- battery below F_B, m -->

      <!-- Rotor aerodynamic coefficients (paper Table 1, Eq. 24-25) -->
      <k_f>5.2400e-6</k_f>                <!-- f_p = k_f * gamma_dot^2, kg m -->
      <k_tau>1.0800e-8</k_tau>            <!-- tau_p = k_tau * gamma_dot^2, kg m^2 -->
      <!--<k_ratio>2.0611e-3</k_ratio>-->        <!-- k = k_tau / k_f, m; tau_p = k * f_p -->

      <!-- Body aerodynamic drag (paper Eq. 31-33), 8-fin prototype -->
      <K_z>2.80908e-5</K_z>               <!-- tau = -sign(wz) * K_z * wz^2 -->

      <!-- Rotor inertia for the gyroscopic term (paper Eq. 44) -->
      <J_pz>1.04e-5</J_pz>                <!-- CORRECTED to 2*J_pd (triangle-inequality
                                               equality); Table 1 prints 1.061e-4 -->
      <J_pd>5.2e-6</J_pd>                 <!-- unused by the wrench; kept for reference -->

      <!-- Masses (paper Table 1). Redundant with <inertial> above; the plugin
           should CHECK them against the resolved link masses and warn on
           mismatch rather than use them. -->
      <m_b>0.2100</m_b>
      <m_s>0.2007</m_s>
      <m_p>0.0067</m_p>

      <!-- Control set limits (paper Sec. 7.1, Eq. 80) -->
      <alpha_max_deg>30.0</alpha_max_deg> <!-- tilt cone half-angle -->
      <lambda_max>1.2</lambda_max>        <!-- max gamma_dot / Omega_hover -->
      <Omega_hover>945.0</Omega_hover>    <!-- paper Table 2; but sqrt(m g / k_f)
                                               with Table 1 values gives 883.8.
                                               See notes. -->

      <!-- Actuator dynamics. First-order placeholder; the tilt channel is
           physically second-order and rotor-speed-scheduled. -->
      <tau_thrust>0.05</tau_thrust>
      <tau_tilt>0.03</tau_tilt>

      <!-- Cosmetic rotor animation. visual_spin_rate is a DISPLAY rate, not
           gamma_dot: rendering 945 rad/s at 60 fps only produces strobing.
           visual_tilt_gain must default to 1.0 — exaggeration is for demos, and
           anything you believe while it is >1 is suspect. -->
      <visual_spin_rate>25.0</visual_spin_rate>
      <visual_tilt_gain>1.0</visual_tilt_gain>

    </plugin>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         IMU PLUGIN

         Replaces gz-sim-imu-system + the commented-out <sensor type="imu"> block above.
         Reads base_link's rigid-body kinematics from the ECM each physics step, converts
         to specific force + body-frame angular velocity, applies noise, and publishes
         directly to the topic PX4's GZBridge::subscribeImu() hardcodes, see
         PX4-Autopilot/src/modules/simulation/gz_bridge/GZBridge.cpp. That topic string
         does not depend on anything in this file and must not be "tidied" to look like a
         real sensor-tree path.
         ================================================================== -->
    <plugin filename="gz_imu_realism_plugin" name="monospinner::ImuPhysical">
      <link_name>base_link</link_name>
      <topic>/world/monospinner_default/model/monospinner/link/base_link/sensor/imu_sensor/imu</topic>
      <update_rate>250</update_rate>
      <!-- Values model the IIM-42653 (TDK DS-000529), the IMU PX4's x500 baseline uses.

           NOISE (white, per sample): datasheet total RMS noise at 100 Hz bandwidth 
           gyro 0.05 deg/s-rms, accel 0.70 mg-rms (Z; X/Y are 0.65 mg, this plugin has one
           stddev per channel type so the larger is used). Per-sample stddev, so these hold
           only near update_rate = 250 Hz; change the rate and they no longer match the
           datasheet's noise density (5 mdeg/s/sqrt(Hz), 70 ug/sqrt(Hz)). -->
      <gyro_noise_stddev>0.0008726646</gyro_noise_stddev>
      <accel_noise_stddev>0.00686</accel_noise_stddev>

      <!-- BIAS (Gauss-Markov; ImuPhysical starts it at a random draw of this stddev, so it
           is also the turn-on offset). stddev = datasheet initial tolerance, taken as
           1-sigma, i.e. an UNCALIBRATED sensor PX4 SITL runs no gyro/accel calibration,
           so EKF2 has to estimate all of it (within EKF2_GBIAS_INIT 0.1 rad/s and
           EKF2_ABIAS_INIT 0.2 m/s^2 defaults).
             gyro:  initial ZRO tolerance +-0.5 deg/s = 0.00873 rad/s (IIM-42653).
             accel: initial zero-g tolerance +-20 mg = 0.196 m/s^2. NOT confirmed for the
                    IIM-42653 this is the IIM-42652 sister part's figure.
           correlation_time is an ENGINEERING ESTIMATE, not a datasheet number: drift is
           mostly thermal (ZRO tempco +-0.04 deg/s/degC), and 1000 s gives ~0.17 deg/s of
           drift over a 60 s flight, about a 4 degC change. -->
      <gyro_bias_stddev>0.00873</gyro_bias_stddev>
      <gyro_bias_correlation_time>1000</gyro_bias_correlation_time>
      <accel_bias_stddev>0.196</accel_bias_stddev>
      <accel_bias_correlation_time>1000</accel_bias_correlation_time>

      <!-- SATURATION: the full-scale range PX4's driver configures, GYRO_FS_SEL_4000_DPS /
           ACCEL_FS_SEL_32G (PX4-Autopilot/src/drivers/imu/invensense/iim42653/
           IIM42653.hpp). 4000 deg/s = 69.81 rad/s; 32 g = 313.8 m/s^2. -->
      <gyro_saturation>69.81</gyro_saturation>
      <accel_saturation>313.8</accel_saturation>

      <!-- Fixes the noise/bias RNG so runs are reproducible; ImuPhysical defaults to this
           same value (0) if <seed> is omitted, but it's set explicitly here so it's visible
           without reading the plugin source. Change it to get an independent noise draw. -->
      <seed>0</seed>

      <!--debugging-->
      <publish_ground_truth>true</publish_ground_truth>
      <ground_truth_topic>/model/monospinner/imu_gd_truth</ground_truth_topic>


    </plugin>
    <!-- ==================================================================
         MAGNETOMETER PLUGIN

         Replaces the commented-out <sensor type="magnetometer"> block above (gz-sim 8's
         magnetometer is wrong under tilt, see the notes there). Rotates a constant world
         field into base_link with the true attitude, adds Gauss-Markov bias + white noise,
         and publishes on the topic GZBridge::subscribeMag() hardcodes
         (PX4-Autopilot/src/modules/simulation/gz_bridge/GZBridge.cpp:230-231), pre-swapped
         so GZBridge's axis swap yields the correct FRD body field. Mounted with no rotation
         relative to base_link: no compensating yaw is needed any more.
         ================================================================== -->
    <plugin filename="gz_magnetometer_realism_plugin" name="monospinner::MagnetometerPhysical">
      <link_name>base_link</link_name>
      <topic>/world/monospinner_default/model/monospinner/link/base_link/sensor/magnetometer_sensor/magnetometer</topic>
      <update_rate>100</update_rate>

      <!-- WORLD FIELD, gz world ENU (East North Up), gauss. Must equal what EKF2 expects:
           PX4's own WMM (PX4-Autopilot/src/lib/world_magnetic_model, WMM-2020 tables),
           which EKF2 evaluates at the GPS position (aid_sources/magnetometer/
           mag_control.cpp:629-658, default EKF2_DECL_TYPE). Computed by compiling PX4's
           geo_mag_declination.cpp at this world's <spherical_coordinates>
           (lat 47.397971, lon 8.546164):
             declination +3.4058 deg, inclination 63.1575 deg, strength 0.48239 gauss
             NED = (0.21743, 0.01294, 0.43041)  ->  ENU = (E, N, -D) below.
           Location-dependent: recompute if the world's lat/lon changes. -->
      <world_magnetic_field>0.01294 0.21743 -0.43041</world_magnetic_field>

      <!-- NOISE (white, per sample, per axis): 3 mgauss RMS, the IIS2MDC/LIS2MDL datasheet
           RMS noise figure (from memory of the ST datasheet, not re-checked here). The
           x500 model's comment quotes the same 3 mgauss but sets 0.0001. -->
      <magnetometer_noise_stddev>0.003</magnetometer_noise_stddev>

      <!-- BIAS (Gauss-Markov). Models the RESIDUAL offset of a calibrated sensor (hard-iron
           and offset calibration leave a few mgauss), not the raw +-60 mgauss zero-gauss
           offset of an uncalibrated IIS2MDC: PX4 assumes a calibrated magnetometer and only
           learns small biases in flight. 5 mgauss is about 1 % of the field (~1.3 deg of
           heading on the 0.217 gauss horizontal component). The bias starts at zero.
           correlation_time is an ENGINEERING ESTIMATE (thermal drift), matched to the
           IMU plugin's 1000 s. -->
      <magnetometer_bias_stddev>0.005</magnetometer_bias_stddev>
      <magnetometer_bias_correlation_time>1000</magnetometer_bias_correlation_time>
    </plugin>
    <!-- ==================================================================
         GROUND-TRUTH ODOMETRY PUBLISHER  (logging only, no physics)

         Passive observer for scripts/record_vehicle_state.py. Publishes the model's
         world pose and twist as gz.msgs.Odometry. Nothing subscribes to it inside the
         simulation removing this plugin changes no dynamics.

         WHY IT EXISTS: the alternative ground-truth source, the scene broadcaster's
         /world/<w>/dynamic_pose/info, runs at ~60 Hz (GUI-oriented) against a 1 kHz
         physics step. Differentiating a 60 Hz pose stream to recover the body rate of a
         vehicle that spins continuously by design aliases badly.

         WHAT IT IS NOT: this plugin does NOT read true velocity off the ECM. Verified
         against the installed binary its only state accessor is gz::sim::worldPose(),
         and it carries OdometryPublisherPrivate::CalculateAngularVelocity(Pose3 const &,
         Pose3 const &, duration), i.e. it differences two poses over dt exactly as the
         recorder used to. The gain here is entirely from <odom_publish_frequency>: at the
         physics rate the differencing window is one 1 ms step instead of ~16 ms, which is
         what removes the aliasing.

         For ANGULAR RATE specifically, prefer ImuPhysical's <ground_truth_topic> above:
         that plugin calls Link::WorldAngularVelocity() (src/gz_imu_realism_plugin/src/
         ImuPhysical.cc) and publishes it there with no noise or bias. Do not use its
         noisy <topic> as a reference it carries the configured gyro bias.
         Use this plugin for position and linear velocity.

         <dimensions> must be 2 or 3; the plugin errors out if it is missing or anything
         else. Element names verified against the installed
         libgz-sim8-odometry-publisher-system.so, not guessed.
         ================================================================== -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-odometry-publisher-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::OdometryPublisher">
      <dimensions>3</dimensions>
      <robot_base_frame>base_link</robot_base_frame>
      <odom_frame>monospinner/odom</odom_frame>
      <!-- Matches <physics><max_step_size>1ms in worlds/monospinner_default.sdf, so the
           pose-differencing interval is a single physics step. -->
      <odom_publish_frequency>1000</odom_publish_frequency>
      <!-- Set explicitly rather than relying on the default "/model/" + name +
           "/odometry", so the recorder and this file cannot drift apart. -->
      <odom_topic>/model/monospinner/odometry</odom_topic>
    </plugin>
  </model>
</sdf>